{
  "id": "chem-002",
  "title": "Why does ice float on water?",
  "category": "chemistry",
  "subject_area": "water and molecules",
  "difficulty_level": "introductory",
  "target_audience": [
    "general public",
    "primary school",
    "secondary school"
  ],
  "central_question": "Why does a solid like ice float on its own liquid, when most solids sink in their liquid?",
  "template": "principle",
  "principle_statement": "Water is unusual: hydrogen bonds hold ice in an open, ordered structure that is less dense than liquid water, so ice floats.",
  "domain_of_validity": "Ordinary water ice floating on liquid water near freezing, at normal pressure.",
  "scientific_explanation": {
    "level_1": "Most solids sink in their own liquid, but ice floats on water. Water molecules stick to each other with special links called hydrogen bonds. When water freezes, these links lock the molecules into a neat, open pattern with gaps between them. That takes up more space, so ice is less dense than liquid water, and less dense things float.",
    "level_2": "Each water molecule is one oxygen atom bonded to two hydrogen atoms, and neighbouring molecules attract each other through hydrogen bonds. In liquid water these bonds keep forming and breaking. Below about 4 degrees Celsius the molecules move so little that the bonds form more often than they break, and an ordered, hexagonal structure appears. This structure is more spaced out than the disordered liquid, so ice is less dense than liquid water and floats.",
    "level_3": "Density is mass divided by volume, rho = m / V. When water freezes the mass stays the same, but the open hexagonal structure takes up a larger volume, so the density of ice is lower than the density of liquid water. An object that is less dense than the fluid it is in floats. For comparison, solid argon would sink in liquid argon, as the solids of most other substances sink in their own liquids."
  },
  "supporting_evidence": [
    "Ice cubes float in a glass of water.",
    "Solid argon would sink in liquid argon, and so would the solids of most other substances."
  ],
